+**`focusY` and `zoom` are per-window overrides on each `splitInserts[]` entry**,
+and on a multi-take cut you will need them: measured on one real edit the
+hair-top moved ~170px between takes, so a single value cropped the high takes
+and left a dead gap under the seam on the low ones. Two rules, both learned by
+getting them wrong first:
+
+- **Set it from the WORST frame, not an average** — the frame inside that window
+  where the head is highest. Find it from `face_track.py`'s points (5th
+  percentile of y, not the minimum; the tracker drops the face on ~20% of frames
+  and a hand can win the minimum).
+- **Do not compute it, measure it.** Deriving `focusY` from a hair-top detector
+  is unreliable — a dark doorway behind the speaker and moving hands both defeat
+  a threshold, and two calibration points disagreed by 75px on a real clip.
+  Render the worst frame, crop from the seam down (`crop=1080:200:0:<seam>`) with
+  `drawgrid=h=20`, read the headroom, and correct:
+  `focusY_new = focusY - (target - measured) / zoom`. **~50–60px of headroom**
+  reads as framed; 0–15px reads as decapitated. One correction round converges.
+
+**The hook does not automatically take the seam padding.** `hook.paddingTop` 738
+assumes a split is UP during the hook. If your first window opens later, the hook
+renders full-frame and that value drops the headline onto the speaker's face —
+leave the default.
+
 The two are opposites in one specific way, and it is the whole trick: the source
 has a lot of headroom above the head. `top` has to zoom in to throw that headroom
 away; `bottom` **keeps** it, and that is what puts the face under the frame edge
